{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>But if you refrain from vowing.</b> There is no need to say that if you refrain from vowing there is no sin in it; rather, it attaches above to 'when you vow' — that is, and if you refrained from the outset from vowing, there will be no sin in you, for the vow causes sin, namely the sin of delay; and the reading of the verse shall be: in 'do not delay to pay it' — 'and there will be sin in you' if you delay; 'but if you refrain from vowing, there will be no sin in you' — of delay. And likewise one who vows causes many sins besides the sin of delay — either because he cannot fulfil it, or because his intention at the time of the vow is not entirely for the sake of Heaven." "<b>And thus</b> our Rabbis of blessed memory said at the beginning of the first chapter of Ḥullin: the best of both is one who does not vow at all, but rather brings a lamb to the Temple court and slaughters it. And they further said: whoever vows, even though he fulfils it, is called a sinner. What is the verse? ״וכי תחדל לנדור לא יהיה בך חטא״ — hence if he did not refrain, there is sin."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:40.025298419Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}